\DOC X86_UNDEFINED_CHOOSE_TAC

\TYPE {X86_UNDEFINED_CHOOSE_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Strips one leading existential quantifier from the antecedent of an implication
goal, replacing the bound variable by a freshly generated constant-like name.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes no arguments. The goal's conclusion must be an implication
whose antecedent is an existentially quantified equation between a state
expression and a state variable. The outermost existential binder is removed
and its variable instantiated by a new freshly named term inside the body; the
rest of the conclusion and all assumptions are unchanged.
{
       A ?- (?x1 ... xn. t) ==> u
     ============================  X86_UNDEFINED_CHOOSE_TAC
       A ?- (?x2 ... xn. t') ==> u
}
\noindent Here {t'} is {t} with {x1} replaced by the new name; when only one
binder was present (n = 1) the antecedent is left quantifier-free as a plain
equation.

\FAILURE
Fails when the implication's antecedent is a plain equation with no leading
existential quantifier, so that there is no existential binder available to
strip; the failure arises inside the tactic's own rewriting step.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-binder-stripping behaviour, always on
goals whose antecedent begins with an existential quantifier.

\ENDDOC
